items.content:
@apg______ @TPurity_Alphas Exactly.
Largely because they had no interest in dominating anyone, or anything except the mechanisms of nature.
As I said, intelligence is not correlated with a desire to dominate, even within the human species.

@ppeiris @TPurity_Alphas Mark did not "boss me around" and did not "give me the boot" either.
The reliance on, and the high value given to, brilliant innovators with specialized knowledge is what has made the success of Meta, the tech industry, Silicon Valley, the American R&D ecosystem, and American universities.
AI is not going to change that.

@anliakho @TPurity_Alphas 1. Such AI does not exist today.
2. The is no single scale to measure of intelligence. So, expressions like "orders of magnitude smarter" are at best notional.
3. Entities can be intelligent in some domains and very stupid in most others. A perfect example of this is Elon: he certainly has some intelligence when it comes to business and some aspects of technology, but very, very little when it comes to politics or human relationships.

@ZalinskyS @TPurity_Alphas That was supposed to make the point that intelligence is not correlated with a desire for control or dominance, even within the human species.
So, expecting AI to control or dominate humans just because they are intelligent is nonsense. 
But this went over your head and I had to ELI5 you.

